WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.239 --> 00:00:01.360
What is Yusref?

1
00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.829
The Uref hook and react

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.710
allows you to persistently store

3
00:00:05.889 --> 00:00:07.190
a mutable value

4
00:00:07.369 --> 00:00:09.189
that does not cause a re-render

5
00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:10.289
when it is updated.

6
00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.750
It can be used to directly access a DOM element

7
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.049
or store any mutable value.

8
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:17.549
Why do we use

9
00:00:17.709 --> 00:00:18.290
Ysref?

10
00:00:19.239 --> 00:00:21.620
To access Dom elements directly.

11
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:25.969
To store previous state values without triggering re-renders.

12
00:00:26.729 --> 00:00:28.389
To keep any mutable value

13
00:00:28.579 --> 00:00:31.059
that does not need to cause re-renders upon update.

14
00:00:31.969 --> 00:00:33.500
How does use ref work?

15
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.220
Use ref is similar to instance variables in class components.

16
00:00:38.549 --> 00:00:41.930
It provides a container whose current property is mutable

17
00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:43.610
and can hold any value,

18
00:00:43.729 --> 00:00:45.669
like instance fields and classes.

19
00:00:46.389 --> 00:00:47.610
The use ref hook

20
00:00:47.729 --> 00:00:49.569
is initialized with a value

21
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.889
and returns a mutable ref object.

22
00:00:52.389 --> 00:00:53.150
For example,

23
00:00:53.639 --> 00:00:56.110
const my ref equals use ref

24
00:00:56.319 --> 00:00:57.349
with an initial value.

25
00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.610
Accessing Dom elements.

26
00:01:00.389 --> 00:01:03.310
One common use of Uref is to access DOM elements directly.

27
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:09.610
For example,

28
00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:10.389
type

29
00:01:10.699 --> 00:01:11.839
const my input

30
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:13.680
equals use ref with null.

31
00:01:14.059 --> 00:01:15.150
Then define a function,

32
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.040
function focus input.

33
00:01:17.379 --> 00:01:18.010
Inside,

34
00:01:18.379 --> 00:01:20.779
type my input.curr.focus.

35
00:01:21.449 --> 00:01:22.330
In this example,

36
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.349
my input is assigned to an element's ref attribute,

37
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.790
and calling focus input will focus that element.

38
00:01:28.839 --> 00:01:30.209
Storing previous values.

39
00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.750
Another use of URF is to keep track of previous state values.

40
00:01:35.169 --> 00:01:38.550
This is useful in scenarios where you need to compare the current state value

41
00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.489
with its previous value without causing extra renders.

42
00:01:49.069 --> 00:01:49.889
For example,

43
00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:51.790
const prev count ref

44
00:01:51.949 --> 00:01:53.519
equals use ref.

45
00:01:53.989 --> 00:01:55.209
Inside use effect,

46
00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:55.949
type

47
00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.230
prevcount ref.current equals count.

48
00:02:00.169 --> 00:02:00.529
Here,

49
00:02:00.809 --> 00:02:03.230
Prevcount ref stores the previous value of count.

50
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.910
We will explore both examples in more detail later.

51
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.699
See you in the next lesson.